# -x (do not cross a mount point) is an extension of macOS and GNU; the spec has no such option.
-x a x
# -r: no longer in the spec ("may be present in some implementations"); PCTS still runs cp -r. Ours is -R
# (links are copied as links, special files are recreated), like GNU. macOS -r follows every link, so it
# fails on the dangling link in the hierarchy.
-r d x
-rp d x
-r d tgt
# A directory copied into itself or into something below itself (the destination d/d, d/new, ... lies in
# the hierarchy being copied). The spec does not say. macOS copies what was there at the start and says
# yes; GNU makes the directory and then refuses; ours refuses before anything is made.
-R d ld
-R d d
-R d d/new
-R d d/sub/new
# The same class, found by the corpus: the destination is the directory itself spelled another way
# (d/., d/../d), a directory below it (d/sub), a link to it (ld), or the current directory is the source
# (d/.. into x). With -L through a link that leads back (hi/ld -> ../odir, copied into odir) GNU does not
# stop at all but goes down hi/ld/hi/ld/... until the name is too long; ours stops at the first return.
# Every line was looked at: both exit 1, and what differs is only what GNU has below the destination.
-R d d/.
-R d d/../d
-R d d/sub
-R d/sub d/sub
-R d/sub d/sub/new
-R d/.. x
-RL d ld
-RL hi odir
-RL hi hi/new
-RL cy cy/sub/deep/new
-R unr unr
-R 'dir 😀' 'dir 😀'
-R d d/
-R d ./d
-RL ld d
-R d d/empty
-R d d/sublink
-RL lp x
-RL lp tgt
-R unr unr/new
-R 'dir 😀' 'dir 😀/sub'
